WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:03.835
SQL Server can now accept
Entra ID tokens on premises,

2
00:00:03.835 --> 00:00:06.587
through Azure Arc, instead of Kerberos.

3
00:00:06.587 --> 00:00:09.001
That sounds like it removes certificates.

4
00:00:09.001 --> 00:00:09.887
It doesn't.

5
00:00:09.887 --> 00:00:13.219
Around one SQL Server there are
three different credentials,

6
00:00:13.219 --> 00:00:14.787
with three different owners.

7
00:00:14.787 --> 00:00:19.137
The TLS certificate, which CyberArk
Certificate Manager renews.

8
00:00:19.137 --> 00:00:25.124
On SQL Server 2022, an app-registration
certificate that lives in Key Vault.

9
00:00:25.124 --> 00:00:30.346
And on SQL Server 2025, a managed
identity that the Arc agent looks after,

10
00:00:30.346 --> 00:00:33.000
with no certificate to manage at all.

11
00:00:33.000 --> 00:00:35.761
The next slide explains
why the TLS certificate

12
00:00:35.761 --> 00:00:37.970
suddenly matters more than it used to.
